Self value (scalar)
description: the self-value's types - JSON Schema's set, binary added, its own subtyping

- >
  Each omnivorous node can have a self value (or scalar). This value belongs to one of these types

- !!<*yamlover: $defs: table>
  header:
    - Yamlover name
    - JSON Schema name
    - Type description
      width: 2
    - Examples
  - - str
    - string
    - 'Unicode text. Quoting forces it - `''1''` is the string, bare `1` is not. Refined by `format`; a bare string''s default prose format is `text/marklower`.'
    - '`Hello, *world*` · `''42''`'
  - - int
    - integer
    - 'A whole number - the subtype of `float` whose value has no fractional part.'
    - '`30` · `-7` · `0`'
  - - float
    - number
    - 'Any number, whole or fractional. JSON Schema calls the same type `number`; `int` is its subtype.'
    - '`3.14` · `-2.5` · `6.02e23`'
  - - bool
    - boolean
    - 'The two truth values - nothing else coerces to them.'
    - '`true` · `false`'
  - - 'null'
    - 'null'
    - 'The singleton - its only value is `null`, so the type is also a value. A bare key `a:` means `a: null`.'
    - '`~` · `null` · a bare `a:`'
  - - binary
    - '- (none)'
    - 'Opaque bytes - the yamlover addition; JSON Schema has no bytes type. Refined by `format` (`image/png`, `int32/le`); usually stored as a file, and YAML''s `!!binary` (base64) is the same value.'
    - 'the bytes of `logo.png` · `!!binary R0lGODlh...`'


- 'The self-value''s type keeps JSON Schema''s set. It is not flat - it subtypes:'
- !!<*yamlover: $defs: bullets>
  - '`null` - the **singleton**: its only value is null (`~`, or a bare `a:`). A type that is also a value.'
  - '`bool` · `float` · `int` - with `int` a subtype of `float` (a number whose value is whole).'
  - '`str` - refined by `format` (`text/markdown`, `text/x-latex`, ...); a bare string''s default prose format is `text/marklower`.'
  - '`binary` - the yamlover addition: opaque bytes, refined by `format` (`image/png`, `application/pdf`, `int32/le`). YAML''s `!!binary` (base64) is the same value.'
  - '`leaf` - a node with **no members**: a value alone. The alias covering every type above.'
- >
  `format` is **not a type** - it refines a facet, usually the value facet, and has an open
  sub-lattice of its own (whether `text/markdown` subtypes `text/plain` is deliberately unsettled).
  Closed `leaf` - a node with no members - and the *open* value facet differ exactly on omni nodes:
  the open reading includes a value that has grown entries.
- 'YAML''s extra collection "types" are refinements of the facets, not new corners:'
- !!<format: text/plain> |
  binary     → {value: {type: binary, format: image/png},
                keyed: false, ordinal: false}    # value axis
  timestamp  → {value: {type: string, format: date-time},
                keyed: false, ordinal: false}    # value axis
  set        → {value: false,
                ordinal: {uniqueItems: true}}    # a dedup FLAG
  omap       → {value: false, ordinal: false}    # = map - maps are
                                                 #   ALREADY ordered
  pairs      → {value: false, keyed: false,      # ordered [k, v]s;
                ordinal: {items: ...}}             #   keys may repeat
- >
  A facet carries a small fixed set of modifier keywords beyond false-or-schema - `uniqueItems` (set),
  key-uniqueness (omap versus pairs), ordering (yamlover's default) - almost all kept verbatim from
  JSON Schema. The merge key `<<` and YAML's `!!value` are mechanisms, not types.
- >
  Three of these names are more than facet refinements: `!!omap`, `!!pairs`, and `!!binary` are
  reserved as **transform names** ([transforms](::transform)). The records above are only their
  *result* shapes - the reshaping itself (a mapping to and from ordered `[key, value]` members,
  bytes decoded into a value and back) belongs to the transform layer, to be written out
  explicitly.
